          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-342630",
          ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{2}+2x+1)}^{2},$ the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is{{field-1}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{2},\\,g={x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2g\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =[2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2){]}_{x=0} \\\\  & =2((0{)}^{2}+2\\cdot (0)+1)(2\\cdot (0)+2) \\\\  & =2(1)(2) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
