          ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(3-2x{)}^{3},$ the equation of the tangent at the point $(2,-1)$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=3-2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-6(3-2x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & =(-6{(3-2x)}^{2}){|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-6{(3-2(2))}^{2} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,-1)$ is $m=-6.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-6\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y & =-6x+12-1 \\\\ y & =-6x+11\\end{aligned}\n$$"
