          ":question/problem": "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[({t}^{2}+1)^{3}]=\\,${{field-1}}",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=({t}^{2}+1)^{3}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)={t}^{2}+1,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(t) & =({t}^{2}+1)^{\u2032}=2t, \\\\ {h}^{\u2032}(g(t)) & =({g}^{3})^{\u2032}=3{g}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[({t}^{2}+1)^{3}] & ={h}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{\u2032}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =6t({t}^{2}+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
