      {
        ":db/id": 17592186049317,
        ":topic/id": "ebb1ef31-5cab-5892-affd-92e347cc4f35",
        ":topic/knowledge-points": [
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184340,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186183955,
              ":question/id": "150ffc7c-4e70-5f9d-b8e2-8aceacfbd488",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-14883",
              ":question/problem": "Find $(f \\circ g)(x)$ for $f(x) = 3x^2 - 2x$ and $g(x) = x + 4$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Note that $(f \\circ g)(x)$ is equivalent to $f(g(x))$.\n\nTo find $f(g(x))$, we write down the function $f$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 3x^{2} - 2x \\\\\nf(g(x)) &= 3(g(x))^{2} - 2(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}g(x)} = {\\color{red}x+4}\n$$\n\ninto the right-hand side of the above and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(g(x)) &= 3(g(x))^{2} - 2(g(x)) \\\\\n&= 3(x + 4)^{2} - 2(x + 4) \\\\\n&= 3(x + 4)(x + 4) - 2x - 8 \\\\\n&= 3(x^{2} + 4x + 4x + 16) - 2x - 8 \\\\\n&= 3(x^{2} + 8x + 16) - 2x - 8 \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} + 24x + 48 - 2x - 8 \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} + 22x + 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "8569b3c6-28a0-5a45-902f-c513e6def877",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Finding a Composite Function"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184341,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186183956,
              ":question/id": "64ae97f6-016a-5ebf-87a0-4731c2970c1c",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-14886",
              ":question/problem": "If $g(x)=x^2+x$, then find $(g\\circ g)(x)$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "First, we note that $(g\\circ g)(x)$ is equivalent to $g(g(x))$.\n\nTo find $g(g(x))$, we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= x^{2} + x \\\\\ng(g(x)) &= (g(x))^{2} + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}g(x)} = {\\color{red}x^2+x}\n$$\n\ninto the right-hand side of the above and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(g(x)) &= (g(x))^{2} + g(x) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + x)^{2} + (x^{2} + x) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + x)(x^{2} + x) + x^{2} + x \\\\\n&= (x^{4} + x^{3} + x^{3} + x^{2}) + x^{2} + x \\\\\n&= x^{4} + 2x^{3} + 2x^{2} + x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(g(x)) = x^4+2x^3+2x^2+x\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "8092788d-bb38-5527-8069-8e32c97e08b9",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Composing a Function With Itself"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184342,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186183957,
              ":question/id": "95997b9c-6e35-5763-986a-74233b3dc707",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-14891",
              ":question/problem": "If $f(x) = x^2-1$, then find $f(a+1)$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "To find $f(a+1)$, we write down the function $f$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $(a+1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{2} - 1 \\\\\nf(a + 1) &= (a + 1)^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(a + 1) &= (a + 1)^{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= (a + 1)(a + 1) - 1 \\\\\n&= a^{2} + a + a + 1 - 1 \\\\\n&= a^{2} + 2a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(a+1)=a^2+2a\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "9dd4252e-6408-5e03-828f-d3d94cccaa7c",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Evaluating a Function With a Variable Input"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184343,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186184044,
              ":question/id": "56db49ab-7850-5d9e-ab77-8aa05019521a",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-19768",
              ":question/problem": "If $f(x) = r + 4x$ and $f(5a+1) = 22a$, where $a$ and $r$ are constants, find an expression for $r$ in terms of $a$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "We are given the linear function\n\n$$\nf(x) = r + 4x\n$$\n\nand are told that\n\n$$\nf(5a+1) = 22a\n$$\n\nTo find $f(5a+1)$ in terms of $r$, we write down the function $f$ and replace every occurence of $x$ with $5a+1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= r + 4x \\\\\nf(5a + 1) &= r + 4(5a + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we simplify\n\n$$\nf(5a+1) = 22a\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(5a + 1) &= 22a \\\\\nr + 4(5a + 1) &= 22a \\\\\nr + 20a + 4 &= 22a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $r{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + 20a + 4 &= 22a \\\\\nr &= 22a - 20a - 4 \\\\\nr &= 2a - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "65538924-49b2-597e-a0c2-d4ff34112b2d",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Solving Equations Involving Composite Function"
          }
